parts.text:
The user wants scrolling (mouse wheel) to fluidly rotate the globe to the next country, alphabetical by country. So scrolling cycles through countries alphabetically, rotating the globe to point at each country, and presumably showing its panel.

I need:
1. A list of countries sorted alphabetically (only those with counts? or all countries? Probably only countries with counts — 27 of them). The user says "go alphabetical by country." Likely cycle through the countries that have tweets (the ones colored). Let me use countries with count > 0, sorted alphabetically.

2. Need each country's centroid (lat/lng) to point the globe at it. I can compute centroid from the GeoJSON feature geometry. For polygons/multipolygons, compute centroid. Using d3.geoCentroid would be ideal, but I don't have d3. I can implement a simple centroid: average of coordinates, or use the bounding box center. Better: use area-weighted centroid via spherical? Simpler: compute the centroid of the largest polygon ring's coordinates (average of vertices). For most countries that's okay, but for multipolygons like US (with Alaska/Hawaii) the average could be off. Better to compute centroid of the largest-area polygon by vertex count or by shoelace area, then average its vertices. Or use bbox center of the largest polygon.

Actually globe.gl polygons are 3D meshes; I could get the centroid via three.js? Hard.

Better approach: use `d3-geo`'s geoCentroid via esm.sh import, or use the `topojson` + a simple centroid. Let me just implement a decent centroid:
- For each feature, gather all polygons (for MultiPolygon, coordinates is array of polygons; for Polygon, coordinates is array of rings).
- Pick the polygon with the largest absolute shoelace area (in lon/lat plane).
- Compute its centroid via the polygon centroid formula (shoelace centroid).
That handles US (largest = continental), etc.

Actually simpler and robust enough: use the ring with most points (main landmass) and compute average lon/lat, but handle antimeridian. Given our countries (US, India, Canada, UK, Israel, Germany, etc.), average is fine. US with Alaska could skew west/north; but using largest-area polygon (continental) avoids that.

Let me implement shoelace centroid per polygon and pick largest area.

3. Scrolling: attach wheel event to the globe container. On wheel down, go to next country; wheel up, previous. Debounce/throttle so one gesture = one step. Rotate smoothly: globe.gl's `pointOfView({lat,lng,altitude}, transitionMs)` animates. Also set altitude to something like 1.6 for a closer look, or keep 2.4? "scrolls to the next country by rotating the globe" — so rotate to center the country. Let me use altitude ~1.8 maybe. Keep altitude consistent (e.g., 2.0).

Also update the panel to show that country as we scroll, so it feels like browsing.

4. Prevent the default page scroll (body overflow hidden already).

5. Also disable auto-rotate when scrolling starts? Probably pause auto-rotate while browsing. I'll turn off auto-rotate on first scroll (and uncheck the box) or just let pointOfView override. Auto-rotate would fight the transition. Let me pause auto-rotate when user scrolls, and resume? Simpler: on scroll, disable auto-rotate and uncheck the checkbox.

6. Add a visual indicator of current index/name, e.g., in HUD or a small toast. The panel already shows. Maybe add "3 / 27" indicator.

7. Wheel event with passive:false to preventDefault. Also throttle: accumulate deltaY and step when threshold reached, with a cooldown to avoid fast scroll jumping many countries.

Let me implement a cooldown-based approach: track `lastStep` time; require e.g. 450ms between steps, OR use accumulation. I'll do accumulation with threshold and reset.

Actually a clean approach: on wheel, if now - lastNav < 400ms, ignore. Else step. This gives one step per gesture-ish. But trackpad momentum could fire many events. Cooldown handles it.

Better: accumulate deltaY; when |acc| > threshold (e.g., 100), step and reset acc; and enforce a small cooldown after step.

Let me do accumulation.

Also should I include ALL countries alphabetically or only the ones with data? The user: "go alphabetical by country". Given the viz is about tweets per country, cycling through only countries with tweets makes sense and each has data to show. But "alphabetical by country" might mean all countries. Hmm. I think cycling through the 27 with data is most useful; otherwise you'd scroll through 175 empty countries. I'll go with countries that have count > 0, sorted alphabetically. I'll mention it.

Let me also handle: on nav, highlight the country (like hover) and show panel. I'll set a "selected" country and update cap colors accordingly.

Currently hover changes colors. I'll add a `selected` variable and incorporate into cap color: selected → highlight color (e.g., '#ffd23f' or a distinct selection color). Let me use a distinct color for selected, maybe white or bright. Hover uses '#ffd23f'. Let me make selected a bright cyan '#4dd0e1' or keep gold. I'll use selected = '#ffd23f' and hover = lighter '#fff2b0'? Let me define: selectedCountry name; cap color function: if selected → '#ffd23f'; else hover handled separately. To avoid conflict, I'll compute in the color function.

Let me restructure: define `capColor(f)` that returns:
- if f.properties.name === selected → '#ffd23f'
- else colorFor(count)
And hover handler temporarily overrides via a `hovered` variable too. Simpler: keep a module-level `hovered` and `selected`, and capColor uses both. Then no need to set polygonCapColor in hover handler (just update the vars and call .polygonCapColor(capColor)). Actually globe.gl doesn't re-render unless the accessor is set again. Setting the same function reference may not trigger update. In the example they call `.polygonCapColor(...)` again to trigger. I'll define capColor and call `world.polygonCapColor(capColor)` after changing vars.

For altitude, similarly define altColor.

Let me write the updated script.

Now computing centroids. Let me compute in the fetch .then after features loaded. Implement:

```
function ringArea(ring){
  let a=0;
  for(let i=0,j=ring.length-1;i<ring.length;j=i++){
    a += (ring[j][0]*ring[i][1]) - (ring[i][0]*ring[j][1]);
  }
  return a/2; // signed
}
function ringCentroid(ring){
  let a=0,cx=0,cy=0;
  for(let i=0,j=ring.length-1;i<ring.length;j=i++){
    const f = ring[j][0]*ring[i][1] - ring[i][0]*ring[j][1];
    a += f;
    cx += (ring[j][0]+ring[i][0])*f;
    cy += (ring[j][1]+ring[i][1])*f;
  }
  a = a/2;
  if (Math.abs(a) < 1e-9) { // degenerate; fallback average
    let sx=0,sy=0; for(const p of ring){sx+=p[0];sy+=p[1];}
    return [sx/ring.length, sy/ring.length];
  }
  return [cx/(6*a), cy/(6*a)];
}
function featureCentroid(f){
  const polys = f.geometry.type === 'Polygon' ? [f.geometry.coordinates] : f.geometry.coordinates;
  let best=null, bestArea=0;
  for(const poly of polys){
    const outer = poly[0];
    const area = Math.abs(ringArea(outer));
    if(area>bestArea){bestArea=area;best=poly;}
  }
  const ring = best ? best[0] : null;
  if(!ring) return [0,0];
  const [lng,lat] = ringCentroid(ring);
  return [lng,lat];
}
```

Note: coordinates are [lng, lat]. globe.gl pointOfView wants {lat, lng}. Good.

Antarctica filtered out already. Some features may have geometry null (e.g., some have no geometry) — guard.

Now the nav list:
```
const NAV = Object.keys(counts).filter(n=>counts[n]>0).sort((a,b)=>a.localeCompare(b));
```
But we need centroids keyed by name: build map name->[lng,lat] from features.

Then:
```
let idx = -1;
function go(i){
