parts.text:
Two changes:
1. Remove the Locations section from the panel card.
2. Fix scroll: currently momentum scrolls cause multiple steps. They want one scroll = one prev/next. So per scroll gesture, only one step. The current cooldown of 650ms should mostly handle it, but trackpad momentum lasts longer. Better approach: detect gesture end and require a new gesture. Common technique: accumulate delta and require a pause (idle) before allowing next step. I.e., use a timeout that resets a "gesture active" flag after e.g. 200ms of no wheel events; only allow a step when not in a gesture.

Approach:
- On wheel: if inGesture (recent events), accumulate but do not step again. 
- Only trigger a step on the first event of a gesture.
- A gesture ends after ~250ms of silence.

Implementation:
```
let gestureActive = false;
let gestureTimer = null;
el.addEventListener('wheel', e => {
  e.preventDefault();
  if (gestureActive) {
    // still same gesture; reset end timer, ignore
    clearTimeout(gestureTimer);
    gestureTimer = setTimeout(() => gestureActive = false, 200);
    return;
  }
  gestureActive = true;
  const dir = e.deltaY > 0 ? 1 : -1;
  flyTo(idx + dir);
  gestureTimer = setTimeout(() => gestureActive = false, 200);
}, {passive:false});
```

This ensures one step per gesture (per burst of wheel events). Momentum events keep the gesture active, so no extra steps. Once events stop for 200ms, next scroll can step again.

But there's a subtlety: a single deliberate scroll might produce one event; fine. A fast continuous scroll of many notches would be treated as one gesture → one step. That's what they asked ("per scroll, only can go to prev or next"). Good.

The 200ms idle threshold: momentum can have gaps? Usually momentum events are frequent (<200ms apart). 200ms is okay. Maybe use 250ms for safety.

Also there's the "hovers on that country then goes to next" — that's the hover highlight from the cursor as the globe rotates under it. As globe rotates, the cursor may pass over countries triggering hover, then next step. The one-step-per-gesture fix addresses multiple steps. The hover flash is inherent but less annoying.

Actually they said "it hovers on that country then goes to the next before i want it to" — meaning it steps twice. So fixing to one step per gesture solves it.

3. Remove locations section: remove HTML sect-title Locations + ul pList, remove CSS for #panel ul/li maybe (tweets don't use them). And remove JS that builds pList.

Let me edit the template.

Remove from panel HTML:
```
    <div class="sect-title">Locations</div>
    <ul id="pList"></ul>
```

Remove CSS blocks for #panel ul, #panel li, #panel li:nth-child, #panel li b, #panel li i, #panel .empty (keep .empty since tweets use it). Actually .empty is used for tweets empty. Keep .empty. Remove ul/li rules.

Remove JS:
```
  const list = document.getElementById('pList');
  const entries = Object.entries(byCountry[name] || {}).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
  if (!entries.length) {
    list.innerHTML = '<div class="empty">No geolocated tweets for this country.</div>';
  } else {
    list.innerHTML = entries.map(([loc, c]) => `<li><b>${loc}</b><i>${c}</i></li>`).join('');
  }
```

Also maybe keep byCountry data (still embedded, unused) — fine, or leave. I'll leave data as is to avoid changing normalize; minor size. Actually by_country is small. Keep.

Change sect-title for tweets: currently "Tweets (N)". Good. Now it's the only section; maybe keep the title. Fine.

